From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: restore the raw HCI socket layer

Samsung's tree comments out most of net/bluetooth/hci_sock.c. The most
important casualty is hci_sock_create(), whose whole body is commented out so
that it is just

	static int hci_sock_create(struct net *net, struct socket *sock,
				   int protocol, int kern)
	{
	    /* ... */
		return 0;
	}

Android never opens an HCI socket (its Bluetooth HAL talks to the MediaTek
stpbt character device), so stock never noticed. Halium does: bluebinder, which
bridges the vendor Bluetooth HAL into BlueZ, calls
socket(AF_BLUETOOTH, SOCK_RAW, BTPROTO_HCI). bt_sock_create() then gets a
success return with sock->sk still NULL and calls bt_sock_reclassify_lock(NULL):

	kernel BUG at net/bluetooth/af_bluetooth.c:71!
	pc : bt_sock_reclassify_lock+0x58/0x5c
	lr : bt_sock_create+0xcc/0x...
	x19: 0000000000000000    (sk)

That line is BUG_ON(!sk), the first check in the function. MediaTek's
ipanic_die()/mrdump_common_die() then resets the device, about 57 s into every
boot, which with the bootloader phase is a 77 s boot loop on the SM-T220. This
was measured from the ramoops console left behind by the looping boot, read in
TWRP from /sys/fs/pstore/console-ramoops-0.

net/bluetooth/ differs from v4.19.191 in this one file only; everything else,
including l2cap_sock.c and sco.c, is identical. The patch makes hci_sock.c equal
to the v4.19.191 version again, so every change is the removal of Samsung's
// and /* */ comments and of the stub "return 0;".

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [restores upstream's own hci_sock.c in a vendor tree that disabled it]
